.TH mbpy_histplot 1 "12 September 2026" "MB-System 5.0" "MB-System 5.0"
.SH NAME
\fBmbpy_histplot\fP \- Create an executable shellscript which will generate
a GMT modern-mode frequency histogram plot of a set of data values.

.SH VERSION
Version 5.0

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmbpy_histplot\fP \fB\-I\fP\fIfile\fP [\fB\-O\fP\fIroot\fP]
[\fB\-\-format\fP\fIfmt\fP]
[\fB\-\-title\fP\fItext\fP]
[\fB\-\-xlabel\fP\fItext\fP]
[\fB\-\-ylabel\fP\fItext\fP]
[\fB\-\-dpi\fP\fIdpi\fP]
[\fB\-X\fP] [\fB\-V\fP]
.br
[\fB\-C\fP[\fIxmin/xmax/\fP]\fIwidth\fP]
[\fB\-G\fP\fIcolor\fP]
[\fB\-W\fP\fIpen\fP]
.br
[\fB\-R\fP\fIxmin/xmax/ymin/ymax\fP]
[\fB\-J\fP\fIspec\fP]
[\fB\-P\fP\fIpagesize\fP]
[\fB\-\-portrait\fP | \fB\-\-landscape\fP]
[\fB\-\-tick\-info\fP\fIspec\fP]
.br
[\fB\-\-gmt\-default\fP\fIparam/value\fP]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmbpy_histplot\fP is a Python/PyGMT-era replacement for the Perl
macro \fBmbm_histplot\fP. It reads a file containing one data value per
line and generates an executable shellscript of \fBGMT\fP \fBmodern
mode\fP commands, built around \fBgmt histogram\fP (the modern-mode
name for classic \fBpshistogram\fP), which produces a frequency
histogram of those values when run: the bars show what percentage of
the data falls in each value bin, and mean / \[+-]1 standard deviation /
\[+-]2 standard deviation normal-distribution curves are always overlaid
(in black, red, and green respectively) for reference.

\fBmbpy_histplot\fP shares its page-layout and projection-fitting
conventions with \fBmbpy_grdplot\fP and \fBmbpy_grd3dplot\fP (and, in
fact, the underlying \fBmbpy_layout.py\fP module), except that its
default projection is a plain linear/Cartesian one (matching
\fBmbm_histplot\fP's own default of \fB\-Jx\fP, rather than the
Mercator/auto-fit default the grid-plotting macros use) -- appropriate
since a histogram's axes are ordinary numeric value/percentage scales,
not geographic coordinates.

Unlike \fBmbm_histplot\fP's \fB\-L\fP\fItitle\fP\fB:\fP\fIxlabel\fP\fB:\fP\fIylabel\fP
colon-joined label option, \fBmbpy_histplot\fP exposes
\fB\-\-title\fP/\fB\-\-xlabel\fP/\fB\-\-ylabel\fP as separate options.

\fBmbpy_histplot\fP does not expose a data-rescaling, xy-overlay, or
custom CPT-file option: \fBmbm_histplot\fP itself refers to variables
for all of these ($data_scale, @xyfiles, $file_cpt) that are never
actually assigned from any command-line option in its own source --
they are leftover, unreachable copy-paste from the \fBmbm_grdplot\fP
template \fBmbm_histplot\fP was originally cloned from, not real
functionality.

\fBNote:\fP classic \fBpshistogram\fP's outline-pen option was
\fB\-L\fP\fIpen\fP; the current \fBgmt histogram\fP module (checked
directly against its own built-in help) has no \fB\-L\fP option at all
and uses \fB\-W\fP\fIpen\fP instead, like nearly every other GMT
plotting module's pen option. \fBmbpy_histplot\fP's own \fB\-W\fP/
\fB\-\-pen\fP option is written out to the generated script using the
current \fB\-W\fP syntax, not the classic \fB\-L\fP mbm_histplot's own
Perl source still emits (which is simply not accepted by a current GMT
installation).

\fBmbpy_histplot\fP requires \fBGMT\fP 6 or later (modern mode) and a
Python 3 interpreter. It depends on \fBmbpy_layout.py\fP (shared with
\fBmbpy_grdplot\fP/\fBmbpy_grd3dplot\fP) and its own
\fBmbpy_histplot_script.py\fP, installed alongside it.

